---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import StepsStrip from '../components/StepsStrip.astro';

const faq = [
  {
    q: 'Why does my iPhone save HEIC at all?',
    a: 'It is a smaller file for the same picture, about half the size of a JPG on the phone. The catch shows up later: forms, shops, older computers and some websites refuse it, and not every program can open it. This page turns it into a JPG (or a PNG or WebP) that opens anywhere.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Will the JPG look the same?',
    a: 'Nearly. A HEIC can hold more colour and brightness range than a JPG can show, so a photo with very bright areas may lose a little of that extra range in the copy. For most photos nothing visible changes. PNG keeps more of the range and the file is bigger; pick PNG above if the photo matters more than the size.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What happens to a Live Photo?',
    a: 'The still picture converts. A Live Photo also carries a short video; that part is not a photo and this page does not extract it. If the file you picked holds one, the result line says so, and only the still was converted.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Does my photo stand the right way up?',
    a: 'Yes. iPhone photos are often stored turned, with a note in the file saying how they stand. This page reads the note, by the file\'s own rotation property or its EXIF note, and the copy comes out upright, the step some converters skip.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Where does my photo go?',
    a: 'Nowhere. The HEIC reader program (about 1.6 MB, loaded once, only when a HEIC file arrives) comes to your browser and the work happens here in the page. Nothing is uploaded.',
  },
];
const faqJson = JSON.stringify({
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: faq.map((f) => ({ '@type': 'Question', name: f.q, acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: f.a } })),
});
const howToJson = JSON.stringify({
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'HowTo',
  name: "How to convert a HEIC photo to a JPG",
  step: [
    { '@type': 'HowToStep', position: 1, name: "Pick a HEIC photo", text: "Drop an iPhone HEIC or HEIF photo on the tray, or click to pick one. It stays on this device. A file in another format is pointed at the batch convert page." },
    { '@type': 'HowToStep', position: 2, name: "Pick the output", text: "JPG unless you have a reason: PNG keeps more of the photo's brightness range and comes out bigger, WebP is the light one for websites. Set the quality for JPG and WebP." },
    { '@type': 'HowToStep', position: 3, name: "Convert to JPG", text: "Press 'Convert to JPG'. The photo is decoded and written here in the page, turned upright if it was stored turned; the result line prints the bytes before and after and anything that changed." },
    { '@type': 'HowToStep', position: 4, name: "Download the JPG", text: "Download the JPG at the dimensions and size the result line states." },
  ],
});
---

<Base title="HEIC to JPG: convert an iPhone HEIC photo to a JPG, free, in your browser · Benchzy" description="Convert one HEIC or HEIF photo (the kind iPhones save) to a JPG, PNG or WebP inside your browser: upright, full size, with the colour change stated before the run. No upload, no account.">
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-5xl px-4">
    <section class="mt-8">
      <p class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-accent-deep">Image tools · convert</p>
      <h1 class="font-display text-4xl font-black mt-2">HEIC to JPG</h1>
      <p class="mt-3 text-lg leading-relaxed max-w-2xl">
        iPhones save photos as HEIC, and then a form, a shop or an older computer refuses
        the file. This page converts one HEIC (or HEIF) photo to a JPG here in your
        browser, at full size, turned upright if it was stored turned. PNG and WebP are
        one tap away on the same ticket if those serve you better, and the exact PNG copy
        has its own door, the <a class="underline underline-offset-4 hover:text-accent-deep" href="/heic-to-png/">HEIC to PNG page</a>. A whole folder of
        mixed photos is the
        <a class="underline underline-offset-4 hover:text-accent-deep" href="/batch-convert-images/">batch convert page</a>,
        which takes HEIC as well.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="ticket mt-6 p-5 sm:p-7" id="hc-tool">
      <div id="hc-tray" class="tray p-6 text-center cursor-pointer" role="button" tabindex="0" aria-label="Pick a HEIC photo">
        <p class="font-display text-xl font-bold">Drop a HEIC photo on the tray, or click to pick one</p>
        <p class="font-mono text-xs text-ink-soft mt-2">HEIC or HEIF in · JPG, PNG or WebP out · it stays on this device</p>
        <input id="hc-file" type="file" accept="image/heic,image/heif,.heic,.heif" class="hidden" />
      </div>
      <p class="file-loaded hidden" data-loaded-for="hc-file" role="status"></p>
      <p class="font-mono text-xs text-ink-soft mt-3">A Live Photo converts as its still picture only; the short video part is not carried into the JPG. The result line says so when a Live Photo is picked.</p>

      <div class="grid gap-6 mt-6 md:grid-cols-[1fr_320px]">
        <div>
          <h2 class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-ink-soft">What changes</h2>
          <p class="text-ink-soft text-sm leading-relaxed mt-3 max-w-md">
            The format changes, and the dimensions do not. Two honest changes ride along,
            stated before you run: a HEIC can hold more brightness range than a JPG can
            show, so a very bright photo may lose a little of that extra range in the copy
            (PNG keeps more of it, and the file is bigger); and if the photo was stored
            turned, the copy comes out upright.
          </p>
          <div id="hc-bg-row" class="mt-5">
            <h3 class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-ink-soft">See-through parts become</h3>
            <div class="flex items-center gap-2 mt-2 flex-wrap">
              <span id="hc-bgs" class="flex gap-2">
                <button type="button" class="chip" data-bg="#ffffff" aria-pressed="true">White</button>
                <button type="button" class="chip" data-bg="#000000" aria-pressed="false">Black</button>
              </span>
              <input id="hc-bg" type="color" value="#ffffff" class="w-12 h-10 border border-ink bg-ticket cursor-pointer" aria-label="Pick the background colour" />
            </div>
            <p class="text-ink-soft text-sm leading-relaxed mt-2 max-w-md">
              JPG cannot hold see-through pixels. Most iPhone photos have none, but if yours
              does, those parts are painted over this colour: white, unless you pick another.
              The result line says when this happened.
            </p>
          </div>
          <img id="hc-preview" alt="The converted result" class="hidden w-full border border-line bg-paper-deep object-contain max-h-72 mt-5" />
        </div>
        <div>
          <h2 class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-ink-soft">Comes out as</h2>
          <div id="hc-formats" class="flex gap-2 mt-2 flex-wrap">
            <button type="button" class="chip" data-format="jpeg" aria-pressed="true">JPG</button>
            <button type="button" class="chip" data-format="png" aria-pressed="false">PNG</button>
            <button type="button" class="chip" data-format="webp" aria-pressed="false">WebP</button>
          </div>
          <div id="hc-quality-row" class="mt-5">
            <label class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-ink-soft block" for="hc-quality">Quality (JPG and WebP)</label>
            <input id="hc-quality" type="number" min="1" max="100" step="1" value="90" class="mt-1 border border-ink bg-ticket px-3 py-2 font-mono w-24" />
            <p class="text-ink-soft text-sm mt-2">Higher keeps more detail and more bytes. 90 is a good all-round setting. PNG has no quality setting: it is written exactly.</p>
          </div>
          <h2 class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-ink-soft mt-5">File spec</h2>
          <dl id="hc-spec" class="mt-2">
            <div class="spec-row"><dt>file</dt><dd>none picked yet</dd></div>
          </dl>
          <button id="hc-run" class="btn-primary w-full mt-5" type="button" disabled>Convert to JPG</button>
          <p id="hc-status" class="font-mono text-sm mt-4 leading-relaxed" aria-live="polite">Pick a HEIC photo to begin.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div id="hc-result" class="receipt hidden mt-8">
        <div class="tear pt-5 flex items-center gap-4 flex-wrap">
          <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-bold">Result</h2>
          <span class="stamp" id="hc-stamp">Done on your device</span>
        </div>
        <p id="hc-achieved" class="mt-3 leading-relaxed max-w-2xl"></p>
        <a id="hc-download" class="btn-primary inline-block no-underline mt-3" href="#" download>Download the JPG</a>
      </div>
    </section>

    <StepsStrip who="For one iPhone photo that has to arrive as a JPG: a form that refuses HEIC, a shop upload, a photo for an older computer, a picture to send someone whose phone is not an iPhone." steps={[{ n: '01', t: 'Pick a HEIC photo' }, { n: '02', t: 'Pick JPG, PNG or WebP and the quality' }, { n: '03', t: 'Download the converted photo' }]} />

    <section class="mt-12 grid gap-8 md:grid-cols-2">
      <div>
        <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-bold border-b-2 border-ink pb-2">How it works</h2>
        <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-ink-soft">
          The photo is decoded by libheif, the open-source HEIC reader (LGPL; the full
          credit, licence and source links are on the
          <a class="underline underline-offset-4" href="/credits/">credits page</a>), and
          written by the same image program the other convert doors run. The first HEIC
          run loads the reader (about 1.6 MB, one time only, and only when a HEIC file
          arrives) and the image program (about 2 MB, one time only). The download is how
          we protect your privacy: the program comes to your device and does the work
          there, so your photo never leaves it.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-bold border-b-2 border-ink pb-2">Honest limits</h2>
        <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-ink-soft">
          The copy is a standard-range picture: the extra brightness information an iPhone
          keeps for its own screen does not fit in a JPG, a PNG or a WebP as written here,
          so keep the HEIC if it is your master copy. A Live Photo's still picture
          converts; the short video that travels with it is a separate thing and is not
          extracted. And this page converts still photos: a HEIC that is only a video
          sequence has no still to give and the page will say it could not read one.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-10 mb-4">
      <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-bold border-b-2 border-ink pb-2">Questions</h2>
      <div class="mt-4 grid gap-4">
        {faq.map((f) => (
          <article class="ticket-soft p-5">
            <h3 class="font-bold">{f.q}</h3>
            <p class="text-ink-soft leading-relaxed mt-1">{f.a}</p>
          </article>
        ))}
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={faqJson}></script>
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={howToJson}></script>
  <script>
    import '../scripts/heic-to-jpg.js';
  </script>
</Base>
